<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yasmah-Addu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yasmah-Addu was the younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, who installed him as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Letters found at Mari show his father rebuking him for idleness while praising his elder brother Ishme-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> at Ekallatum. He married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishhi-addu">Ishhi-Addu</a> of Qatna, and after Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>'s death he lost Mari to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>, heir of the old royal line.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soon after his death Mari fell to Shamshi-Adad, ruler of the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia, who placed his son <b>Yasmah-Addu</b> on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Shamshi-Adad ruled as 'king of the universe', placing his elder son Ishme-Dagan at Ekallatum and his younger son <b>Yasmah-Addu</b> at Mari.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Mari era King Ishhi-Addu allied with Shamshi-Adad and married his daughter to <b>Yasmah-Addu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/qatna">Qatna</a></li></ul></section>
